us-local-pittsburgh	1972	flat	{"rate":0.02}	https://books.google.com/books?id=La4pAAAAIBAJ&pg=PA16&article_id=6733,601338	Secondary source (The Pittsburgh Press, editorial 'Pete's Fiscal Feats', October 18, 1972, p. 16, via Google News Archive): Mayor Flaherty proposed to eliminate the city of Pittsburgh's one per cent wage tax next year [1973]; Pittsburghers would still have to pay the one per cent wage tax collected by the Board of Education for the city's public schools. Resident earned income tax: city 1% + Pittsburgh School District 1%, as ACIR prints for December 31, 1971. (A council plan to rebate part of the 1971 collections in 1972 was blocked by the mayor, per the same editorial.) Single secondary source.	f
us-local-pittsburgh	1973	flat	{"rate":0.01}	https://www.library.unt.edu/gpo/ACIR/Reports/information/m-79.pdf	Resident earned income tax: Pittsburgh School District 1% only. ACIR Significant Features 1973-74 ed. (M-79), Table 150 p. 293, rates at July 1, 1973: Pittsburgh 1.0 with footnote 11 'School district rate. The Pittsburgh city income tax was repealed effective January 1, 1973.'	f
us-local-pittsburgh	1974	flat	{"rate":0.01}	https://books.google.com/books?id=fWQwAAAAIBAJ&pg=PA2&article_id=3770,1808709	Secondary source (Pittsburgh Post-Gazette, 'House Kills Plan On 2% Wage Tax', July 13, 1974, p. 2, via Google News Archive): Pittsburgh repealed its 1 per cent tax last year [1973]; the school board continues the levy (the 1 per cent wage tax the bill would have let school districts double). The Pittsburgh Press ('House Unit OKs City Tax Relief', May 7, 1975, p. 4, https://books.google.com/books?id=OewjAAAAIBAJ&pg=PA4&article_id=2391,2515943) likewise says Pittsburgh repealed its one per cent wage tax in 1973 and the city's schools still have a one per cent tax on residents; the Post-Gazette of November 22, 1975 ('Pete Will Seek 1976 Wage Tax, Water Rate Hike') reports the mayor asking City Council to restore the 1 per cent city wage tax for 1976. Resident earned income tax: Pittsburgh School District 1% only, as ACIR prints for July 1, 1973.	f
us-local-pittsburgh	1975	flat	{"rate":0.01}	https://books.google.com/books?id=OewjAAAAIBAJ&pg=PA4&article_id=2391,2515943	Secondary source (The Pittsburgh Press, 'House Unit OKs City Tax Relief', May 7, 1975, p. 4, via Google News Archive): city residents working in the suburbs became subject to suburban wage taxes when in 1973 Pittsburgh repealed its one per cent wage tax; the city's schools still have a one per cent tax on residents. The Post-Gazette of November 22, 1975 ('Pete Will Seek 1976 Wage Tax, Water Rate Hike', p. 1) reports the mayor asking City Council to restore the 1 per cent wage tax for 1976 (ACIR shows city 1% + school 1% at July 1, 1976). Resident earned income tax: Pittsburgh School District 1% only. Single secondary source for the school rate.	f
