us-local-pittsburgh	1980	flat	{"rate":0.0225}	https://books.google.com/books?id=-BshAAAAIBAJ&pg=PA9&article_id=3069,380922	Secondary source (The Pittsburgh Press, 'Taxpayers Blast Proposed City School Tax Hike', December 2, 1980, p. 9, via Google News Archive): residents already pay a 1.25 percent wage tax to the City of Pittsburgh; the school board's proposed 1981 budget adds a half percent to the earned income tax, so that Pittsburghers would pay 1.5 percent on wages to the school district. The Press of November 13, 1981 ('5-Mill Cut In City School Tax Expected', p. 1, https://books.google.com/books?id=BxovAAAAIBAJ&pg=PA1&article_id=2940,7196691) says the school wage tax was 1 percent in 1980 and was raised to 1.5 percent in 1981. Resident earned income tax for 1980: city 1.25% + Pittsburgh School District 1%, matching ACIR's split at October 1, 1979.	f
