us-state-AR	1970	brackets	{"percentStandardDeduction":{"rate":0.1,"minimum":{"single":0,"marriedJoint":0},"maximum":{"single":1000,"marriedJoint":1000}},"schedules":{"single":[{"from":0,"rate":0.01},{"from":3000,"rate":0.02},{"from":6000,"rate":0.03},{"from":11000,"rate":0.04},{"from":25000,"rate":0.05}]}}	https://www.library.unt.edu/gpo/ACIR/Reports/information/M-57.pdf	ACIR Significant Features of Fiscal Federalism 1970-71 ed. (report M-57), Table 36 (p. 75), rates as of December 31, 1970, i.e. the law for tax year 1970 as it ended; personal exemptions and standard deduction from Table 37 (p. 83) and Table 38 (p. 85). Pre-1971 statutory schedule: 1% on the first $3,000, 2% to $6,000, 3% to $11,000, 4% to $25,000, 5% over $25,000; the 1969 edition (M-50, Table 40, rates as of December 31, 1969) prints the same schedule, exemptions and standard deduction, while the December 31, 1971 edition (m-74) prints the 1%-7% schedule used from 1971. Same schedule for single and joint returns (joint brackets not doubled; two-earner married taxpayers may file separately on the same return). Standard deduction 10% of income up to $1,000 for single and joint returns ($500 maximum for a married person filing a separate return). Personal exemptions are tax credits ($17.50 per taxpayer, $35 on a joint return, $6 per dependent) and are not modelled. Reduced rates for low-income taxpayers (low-income tax tables) not modelled.	f
