us-state-KY	1975	brackets	{"deduction":{"single":500,"marriedJoint":500},"schedules":{"single":[{"from":0,"rate":0.02},{"from":3000,"rate":0.03},{"from":4000,"rate":0.04},{"from":5000,"rate":0.05},{"from":8000,"rate":0.06}]}}	https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=uc1.d0000878348&seq=169	KRS 141.020's history (legislature's statute page): amended 1970 Ky. Acts ch. 216; 1972 Ky. Acts ch. 84, Pt. II, sec. 2; 1974 Ky. Acts ch. 362; 1976 Ky. Acts ch. 77. 1976 Ky. Acts ch. 77 (S.B. 203), Part I, sec. 3 (Acts of the General Assembly 1976, p. 152; HathiTrust uc1.d0000878348, full view) raised the standard deduction of KRS 141.081 to $650 and sec. 8 applies Part I to taxable years beginning after December 31, 1975; the text it replaced allowed $500 when adjusted gross income was $8,000 or more and the optional table of KRS 141.025 below that, and KRS 141.081's history (legislature's statute page) shows no amendment between 1956 and that act. The same act's section 1 reprints KRS 141.020 with no deletions in the rate subsection, so the rates in force before it (after 1974 Ky. Acts ch. 362) were this 2%-6% schedule. So 1975 has the 1973 schedule and $500 standard deduction. Same schedule for every filing status (two-earner married taxpayers may file separately on the same return). Deduction = the $500 standard deduction (in lieu of all deductions except federal income taxes), available when adjusted gross income is at least $8,000; below that taxpayers use the optional tax table; a joint return gets one $500 deduction. Personal exemptions are $20 tax credits per taxpayer/dependent ($40 joint) and are not modelled. Federal income tax paid was deductible (not modelled). Local income taxes are additional. Earlier this entry used a secondary source (University of Kentucky Policy Paper No. 9, 'Indexing Kentucky's Individual Income Tax', 1980, Table 4-1), whose 1975 column prints the same rates but a $650 standard deduction; the act shows $650 began in 1976, so that column reflects