us-state-MS	1970	brackets	{"deduction":{"single":4000,"marriedJoint":6000},"percentStandardDeduction":{"rate":0.1,"minimum":{"single":0,"marriedJoint":0},"maximum":{"single":500,"marriedJoint":1000}},"schedules":{"single":[{"from":0,"rate":0.03},{"from":5000,"rate":0.04}]}}	https://www.library.unt.edu/gpo/ACIR/Reports/information/M-57.pdf	ACIR Significant Features of Fiscal Federalism 1970-71 ed. (report M-57), Table 36 (State Individual Income Taxes: Rates, December 31, 1970, p. 78), Table 37 (personal exemptions, p. 83) and Table 38 (standard deduction, p. 85); a December 31 table describes tax year 1970 as it ended, and the 1969 edition (M-50, December 31, 1969) prints the same rates, exemptions and standard deduction. 3% on the first $5,000 of net income, 4% over $5,000. Same schedule for joint returns (spouses on a combined return may each use the brackets on their own income; not modelled). Deduction = $4,000 single / $6,000 married personal exemption (no dependent exemption printed); standard deduction 10% of income, max $500 single / $1,000 joint ($500 married separate).	f
us-state-MS	1971	brackets	{"deduction":{"single":4000,"marriedJoint":6000},"percentStandardDeduction":{"rate":0.1,"minimum":{"single":0,"marriedJoint":0},"maximum":{"single":500,"marriedJoint":1000}},"schedules":{"single":[{"from":0,"rate":0.03},{"from":5000,"rate":0.04}]}}	https://www.library.unt.edu/gpo/ACIR/Reports/information/m-74.pdf	ACIR Significant Features of Fiscal Federalism 1971-72 ed. (report M-74), Table 91 (State Individual Income Taxes: Rates, December 31, 1971, p. 203), Table 92 (personal exemptions, p. 208) and Table 93 (standard deduction, p. 210); a December 31 table describes tax year 1971 as it ended. 3% on the first $5,000 of net income, 4% over $5,000. Same schedule for joint returns (spouses on a combined return may each use the brackets on their own income; not modelled). Deduction = $4,000 single / $6,000 married personal exemption (no dependent exemption printed); standard deduction 10% of income, max $500 single / $1,000 joint ($500 married separate). Both the exemption and the standard deduction are higher in the July 1, 1973 edition, so 1972 is not recorded.	f
us-state-MS	1972	brackets	{"deduction":{"single":4000,"marriedJoint":6000},"percentStandardDeduction":{"rate":0.1,"minimum":{"single":0,"marriedJoint":0},"maximum":{"single":500,"marriedJoint":1000}},"schedules":{"single":[{"from":0,"rate":0.03},{"from":5000,"rate":0.04}]}}	https://www.lexisnexis.com/hottopics/mscode/	Same law as 1971. The Mississippi Code 1972 Annotated (LexisNexis public-access edition) history notes show no amendment in 1969-1972 to any part of the computation: the rate section, Miss. Code Ann. 27-7-5 (Laws 1968, ch. 580, s. 26, next amended Laws 1982, Ex Sess, ch. 17), the deductions section including the standard deduction, 27-7-17 (Laws 1966, ch. 628, next amended Laws 1973, ch. 504, s. 2), and the exemptions section, 27-7-21 (Laws 1968, ch. 580, s. 27, next amended Laws 1973, ch. 504, s. 3). So 1972 keeps the 1971 schedule, exemptions and standard deduction (see the 1971 entry, from ACIR Significant Features of Fiscal Federalism 1971-72 ed., Tables 91-93); the $4,500 / $6,500 exemptions and 15% standard deduction of the 1973 entry came with Laws 1973, ch. 504. 1971 entry's notes: ACIR Significant Features of Fiscal Federalism 1971-72 ed. (report M-74), Table 91 (State Individual Income Taxes: Rates, December 31, 1971, p. 203), Table 92 (personal exemptions, p. 208) and Table 93 (standard deduction, p. 210); a December 31 table describes tax year 1971 as it ended. 3% on the first $5,000 of net income, 4% over $5,000. Same schedule for joint returns (spouses on a combined return may each use the brackets on their own income; not modelled). Deduction = $4,000 single / $6,000 married personal exemption (no dependent exemption printed); standard deduction 10% of income, max $500 single / $1,000 joint ($500 married separate). Both the exemption and the standard deduction are higher in the July 1, 1973 edition, so 1972 is not recorded.	f
us-state-MS	1973	brackets	{"deduction":{"single":4500,"marriedJoint":6500},"percentStandardDeduction":{"rate":0.15,"minimum":{"single":0,"marriedJoint":0},"maximum":{"single":750,"marriedJoint":1500}},"schedules":{"single":[{"from":0,"rate":0.03},{"from":5000,"rate":0.04}]}}	https://www.library.unt.edu/gpo/ACIR/Reports/information/m-79.pdf	ACIR Significant Features of Fiscal Federalism 1973-74 ed. (report M-79), Table 141 (State Individual Income Taxes: Rates, July 1, 1973, p. 264), Table 142 (personal exemptions, p. 269) and Table 143 (standard deduction, p. 271). 3% on the first $5,000 of net income, 4% over $5,000. Same schedule for joint returns (spouses on a combined return may each use the brackets on their own income; not modelled). Deduction = $4,500 single / $6,500 married personal exemption (dependents $750); standard deduction 15% of income, max $750 single / $1,500 joint ($750 married separate), the same amounts as the 1976 edition.	f
us-state-MS	1974	brackets	{"deduction":{"single":4500,"marriedJoint":6500},"schedules":{"single":[{"from":0,"rate":0.03},{"from":5000,"rate":0.04}]}}	https://web.archive.org/web/20100613171540/http://www.mscode.com/free/statutes/27/007/0005.htm	3% on the first $5,000 of net income, 4% over $5,000. No document prints the schedule for this year; recorded under the statute-history rule: the Wayback copy of Miss. Code 27-7-5 (Mississippi Code of 1972 as amended) lists its amendments as Laws 1968 ch. 580 and next 1982 Ex. Sess. ch. 17 (whose rates apply 'for the calendar year 1983 ... and all taxable years thereafter'), and the ACIR Significant Features tables print this schedule on both sides of the gap. Same schedule for joint returns (spouses on a combined return may each use the brackets on their own income; not modelled). ACIR tables: July 1, 1973 (M-79 Table 141) and July 1, 1976 (1976-77 ed. Vol. II Table 106). Deduction = $4,500 single / $6,500 married personal exemption (dependents $750): the 27-7-21 history (https://web.archive.org/web/20100613172817/http://www.mscode.com/free/statutes/27/007/0021.htm) shows Laws 1973 ch. 504 Sec. 3 and next Laws 1979 ch. 302 (whose amounts start with calendar year 1979), and both ACIR tables print $4,500 / $6,500. Standard deduction 15% of income, max $750 single / $1,500 joint (27-7-17 history, https://web.archive.org/web/20100613172812/http://www.mscode.com/free/statutes/27/007/0017.htm: Laws 1973 ch. 504 and next 1978 ch. 475; same in both ACIR tables), not recorded.	f
