us-state-MT	1989	brackets	{"deduction":{"single":1200,"marriedJoint":2400},"schedules":{"single":[{"from":0,"rate":0.02},{"from":1500,"rate":0.03},{"from":3000,"rate":0.04},{"from":6000,"rate":0.05},{"from":9000,"rate":0.06},{"from":12000,"rate":0.07},{"from":15000,"rate":0.08},{"from":21000,"rate":0.09},{"from":30000,"rate":0.1},{"from":52500,"rate":0.11}]}}	https://www.library.unt.edu/gpo/ACIR/SFFF/SFFF-1990-Vol-1.pdf	Indexed schedule for tax year 1989. Computed from the statute: 15-30-103(2) MCA has the Department of Revenue multiply the base brackets of 15-30-103(1) ($1,000, $2,000, $4,000, $6,000, $8,000, $10,000, $14,000, $20,000, $35,000; rates 2%-11%) by the inflation factor for the taxable year and round the cumulative brackets to the nearest $100; 15-30-112(6) does the same for the $800 personal exemption, rounded to the nearest $10; 15-30-101(8) defines the inflation factor as the consumer price index for June of the taxable year divided by the CPI for June 1980 (MCA 1997 text, mca.legmt.gov; indexing enacted by Initiative 86, approved November 4, 1980, and 15-30-103 not amended again until Ch. 634, L. 1993). CPI-U, U.S. city average, all items, 1967=100, not seasonally adjusted (BLS series CUUR0000AA0): June 1980 247.6. Check against published years: the same computation reproduces every bracket and the exemption printed for 1982 and 1984 (Montana Taxpayers Digest), 1986, 1988, 1990 (ACIR) and 1991-1992; for 1983 it gives $16,900, $24,100 and $42,100 for the top three brackets where the 1983 entry has $16,800, $24,000 and $42,000 (a factor of 1.20 rather than 1.204), and the 1987 Digest reprints the 1984 brackets. June 1989 CPI 371.7, inflation factor 1.5012: brackets $1,500, $3,000, $6,000, $9,000, $12,000, $15,000, $21,000, $30,000, $52,500; exemption $1,200. Corroborated by ACIR Significant Features of Fiscal Federalism 1990 ed. Vol. 1, Table 19 (rates for tax year 1989, compiled October 1989; 'tax brackets reflect 1989 inflation adjustments'), which prints $1,500, $3,000, $6,000, $9,000, $12,000, $21,000, $30,000 and $52,500 as computed, and Table 16's $1,200 exemption; its seventh bracket, printed '12,001-19,000' at 7% followed by '19,001-21,000' at 8%, is the computed $15,000 misprinted. No surtax: the Department of Revenue's 1986-1988 Biennial Report (archive.org biennialreportfomontrich) gives the 10% surtax for tax years 1987 and 1988, and its 1988-1990 report (biennialreportof00montrich) gives a 5% surtax for tax year 1990 only. Single rate table for all filing statuses (married couples filing jointly use the same table on combined income; most file separately on the same form), so no joint schedule. Deduction = personal exemptions only (1 single / 2 MFJ); Montana standard deduction is a percentage of AGI with min/max, not recorded. Deduction for federal income tax paid (itemizers) not modelled.	f
