us-state-NC	1976	brackets	{"deduction":{"single":1000,"marriedJoint":2000},"percentStandardDeduction":{"rate":0.1,"minimum":{"single":0,"marriedJoint":0},"maximum":{"single":500,"marriedJoint":500}},"schedules":{"single":[{"from":0,"rate":0.03},{"from":2000,"rate":0.04},{"from":4000,"rate":0.05},{"from":6000,"rate":0.06},{"from":10000,"rate":0.07}]}}	https://www.library.unt.edu/gpo/ACIR/SFFF/SFFF-1976-1977-V2.pdf	Rates (former G.S. 105-136, repealed by S.L. 1989-728) applied to each individual's net income after exemptions; joint returns were not permitted, so a two-earner couple paid tax on each spouse's income separately and the married figures here are for a one-earner couple. Deduction is the G.S. 105-149 personal exemption ($1,000 single; $2,000 to the spouse with the larger income, per S.L. 1977-309) and percentStandardDeduction the G.S. 105-147(22) optional standard deduction of 10% of income up to $500 (ACIR 1976; S.L. 1981-13 quotes the pre-1980 $500 cap). The Revenue Act of 1979 (S.L. 1979-801) raised these to $1,100 / $2,200 and $550 for taxable years beginning on or after Jan 1, 1980. Dependent exemptions not modelled. ACIR Significant Features of Fiscal Federalism, Table 106 (rates, July 1, 1976) shows the 3%-7% schedule; the July 1980 ACIR table shows it unchanged and the Revenue Act of 1979 did not amend G.S. 105-136.	f
