4 (s. 2): Subsection C for single individuals (0.9% on the first $500 rising to 9.0% over $100,000) and Subsection B for heads of household and married individuals filing joint returns (0.9% on the first $1,000 rising to 9.0% over $200,000; its own brackets, not the single ones doubled). The act prints each bracket as a base amount plus a rate on the excess, and every base amount was checked against the brackets below it. The separate-return schedule (Subsection A) is not modelled. Deduction = federal personal exemptions ($750 each; ACIR Significant Features of Fiscal Federalism 1976-77 ed., vol. 2, Table 107, personal exemptions as of July 1, 1976: New Mexico $750 single, $1,500 joint, the federal amounts); percentStandardDeduction = the federal standard deduction in effect before the Tax Reduction Act of 1975, 15% of income, max $2,000, low-income allowance $1,300 (same edition, Table 108 as of July 1, 1976, footnotes 2 and 3: New Mexico uses the federal tax base, so in effect the federal standard deduction before the 1975 act is adopted, with a low-income allowance). Table 106 of the same edition (rates as of July 1, 1976) prints this single schedule (16 brackets, first $500 to over $100,000) and gives no joint schedule of its own. For 1974 the pre-1975 federal amounts are the federal law of that year. The state's own income tax is deductible in computing the tax (not modelled). Community property state (in general half the community income is taxable to each spouse). Credits and rebates not modelled.	f
