us-state-NY	1970	brackets	{"schedules":{"single":[{"from":0,"rate":0.02},{"from":1000,"rate":0.03},{"from":3000,"rate":0.04},{"from":5000,"rate":0.05},{"from":7000,"rate":0.06},{"from":9000,"rate":0.07},{"from":11000,"rate":0.08},{"from":13000,"rate":0.09},{"from":15000,"rate":0.1},{"from":17000,"rate":0.11},{"from":19000,"rate":0.12},{"from":21000,"rate":0.13},{"from":23000,"rate":0.14}]}}	https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=uc1.b4378127&seq=972	Laws of New York 1968, ch. 246 (HathiTrust uc1.b4378127, full view, seq 972-973) rewrote Tax Law s. 602 with a schedule for taxable years beginning on and after January 1, 1968: 2% of the first $1,000 rising by 1% per $2,000 bracket to 14% over $23,000 (each bracket's base amount checked against the rates). Laws 1970, ch. 1005 (uc1.b4378132, seq 1499-1503) raised the standard deduction and added s. 602(d), the 3% minimum tax, without changing the rates, and the 1977 act cited below lists ch. 246 of 1968 and ch. 1 of 1972 as the only earlier amendments of s. 602. ACIR Significant Features of Fiscal Federalism 1970-71 ed. (M-57), Table 36, rates as of December 31, 1970 (p. 79) prints the same 2%-14% schedule (14% over $23,000). The New York Department of Taxation and Finance's 1995 study 'Progressive Improvement' (Table 1) gives 15% over $25,000 for this year; the statute and ACIR show that bracket began with taxable years ending after December 31, 1971 (Laws 1972, ch. 1, as shown in the prior-law text of Laws 1977, ch. 70), so the study was not followed. No joint schedule: spouses were taxed separately on this schedule, as in this file's later entries. No deduction recorded, as in this file's other years (personal exemptions and an income-based standard deduction applied). Not modelled: the household credit, the minimum tax on tax preference items, and the no-tax threshold for low New York adjusted gross income. 	f
