us-state-OK	1970	brackets	{"deduction":{"single":1000,"marriedJoint":2000},"schedules":{"single":[{"from":0,"rate":0.01},{"from":1500,"rate":0.02},{"from":3000,"rate":0.03},{"from":4500,"rate":0.04},{"from":6000,"rate":0.05},{"from":7500,"rate":0.06}],"marriedJoint":[{"from":0,"rate":0.01},{"from":3000,"rate":0.02},{"from":6000,"rate":0.03},{"from":9000,"rate":0.04},{"from":12000,"rate":0.05},{"from":15000,"rate":0.06}]}}	https://www.library.unt.edu/gpo/ACIR/Reports/information/M-57.pdf	Pre-1971 schedule (the Oklahoma Income Tax Act of 1971 applies only to years after Dec 31, 1970). ACIR Significant Features of Fiscal Federalism 1970-71 ed. (report M-57), Table 36 (pp. 79-80), rates as of December 31, 1970, i.e. the law for tax year 1970 as it ended; the 1969 edition (M-50, Table 40, December 31, 1969) prints the same schedule. Single schedule 1% on the first $1,500 to 6% over $7,500; joint brackets are double the single ones (ACIR note: the income classes shown are for individuals and heads of households, twice as large for joint returns). Federal income tax paid was deductible in computing net income (ACIR 'Federal tax deductible'); not modelled. Deduction is the $1,000 per-person exemption only (Table 37, p. 83; $500 per dependent); the standard deduction (10% of income, $1,000 maximum single or joint, $500 married filing separately; Table 38, p. 85) depends on income and is left out as in later years.	f
