us-state-OK	1981	brackets	{"deduction":{"single":750,"marriedJoint":1500},"schedules":{"single":[{"from":0,"rate":0.005},{"from":1000,"rate":0.01},{"from":2500,"rate":0.02},{"from":3750,"rate":0.03},{"from":5000,"rate":0.04},{"from":6250,"rate":0.05},{"from":7500,"rate":0.06}],"marriedJoint":[{"from":0,"rate":0.005},{"from":2000,"rate":0.01},{"from":5000,"rate":0.02},{"from":7500,"rate":0.03},{"from":10000,"rate":0.04},{"from":12500,"rate":0.05},{"from":15000,"rate":0.06}]}}	https://archive.org/details/micro_IA40706913_0239/page/n7	Method 1 schedule (taxpayer not deducting federal income tax). Method 2 let taxpayers deduct federal income tax and use a steeper schedule up to 17%; not modelled. Oklahoma Tax Commission Annual Report, fiscal year ended June 30, 1982 (Internet Archive state-documents microfiche, PDF page index 7, 'Review of 1981-82 taxes and collections'): Method I 'Single: from 1/2 of 1% of the first $1,000 of taxable income and graduated up to 6% of income over $7,500. Married: from 1/2 of 1% of the first $2,000 ... up to 6% of income over $15,000'; head of household uses married rates; 'Personal exemption: $750 each. Effective with 1982 returns payable in 1983, the personal exemption is $1,000.' So the $750 exemption applied to 1981 returns (filed during FY1981-82); the FY1980-81 report (micro_IA40706909_0384, page n7) states the same schedule and $750. Deduction is the $750 per-person exemption only; the standard deduction (15% of Oklahoma AGI, $1,000 min / $2,000 max; MFS $500-$1,000) depends on income and is left out. Bracket steps are those printed in the 1980 and 1983 ACIR tables (this file's 1980 and 1983 entries); the FY1981-FY1984 Tax Commission reports all describe the same 1/2%-6% over $7,500 schedule and 68 O.S. 2355 was not amended between Laws 1980, c. 288 and Laws 1985, c. 179/200.	f
