COPY capital_gains_tax_years (tax_year, method, rules, source, notes, projected) FROM stdin;
1970	exclusion	{"method":"","excludedShare":0.5,"alternativeRate":0.25,"alternativeLimit":{"single":50000,"marriedJoint":50000},"alternativeExcessRate":0.295,"source":""}	https://www.jct.gov/getattachment/89bb713c-bb3f-4a1d-a987-425856365722/s-16-70-2406.pdf	JCT General Explanation of the Tax Reform Act of 1969 (JCS-16-70) pp. 15, 166-167: half of net long-term gain is included in income; the 25% alternative rate stays for the first $50,000 of long-term gain, and the maximum rate on gain above $50,000 is 29.5% in 1970 (excluding the 1970 income tax surcharge, not modelled). Cross-checked against Form 1040 instructions 1970 (https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-prior/i1040--1970.pdf): 50% of capital gain distributions is taxable; alternative tax computed on Schedule D. The $50,000 applies to individuals generally (married filing separately $25,000, not verified here, not modelled). Add-on minimum tax on the excluded half not modelled. Dividends were ordinary income (only the small dividend exclusion, ignored).	f
1971	exclusion	{"method":"","excludedShare":0.5,"alternativeRate":0.25,"alternativeLimit":{"single":50000,"marriedJoint":50000},"alternativeExcessRate":0.325,"source":""}	https://www.jct.gov/getattachment/89bb713c-bb3f-4a1d-a987-425856365722/s-16-70-2406.pdf	JCS-16-70 pp. 15, 167: 25% alternative rate on the first $50,000 of net long-term gain; maximum rate on gain above $50,000 is 32.5% in 1971. Cross-checked against Form 1040 instructions 1971 (https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-prior/i1040--1971.pdf): 50% of capital gain distributions taxable; alternative tax on Schedule D. Minimum tax and maximum tax on earned income not modelled. Dividends were ordinary income (only the small dividend exclusion, ignored).	f
1972	exclusion	{"method":"","excludedShare":0.5,"alternativeRate":0.25,"alternativeLimit":{"single":50000,"marriedJoint":50000},"source":""}	https://www.jct.gov/getattachment/89bb713c-bb3f-4a1d-a987-425856365722/s-16-70-2406.pdf	JCS-16-70 p. 167: from 1972 the 25% alternative rate applies only to the first $50,000 of net long-term gain; gain above that gets the regular 50% deduction (top effective rate 35%, half of 70%). Rule unchanged until the Revenue Act of 1978 (JCS-7-79 prior-law description: 50% deduction, partial alternative tax of 25% on the first $50,000). Cross-checked against Form 1040 instructions 1972 (https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-prior/i1040--1972.pdf): 50% of capital gain distributions taxable; alternative tax still computed on Schedule D. Minimum tax on preferences and maximum tax on earned income not modelled. Dividends were ordinary income (only the small dividend exclusion, ignored).	f
