1976	exclusion	{"method":"","excludedShare":0.5,"alternativeRate":0.25,"alternativeLimit":{"single":50000,"marriedJoint":50000},"source":""}	https://www.jct.gov/getattachment/89bb713c-bb3f-4a1d-a987-425856365722/s-16-70-2406.pdf	JCS-16-70 p. 167: from 1972 the 25% alternative rate applies only to the first $50,000 of net long-term gain; gain above that gets the regular 50% deduction (top effective rate 35%, half of 70%). Rule unchanged until the Revenue Act of 1978 (JCS-7-79 prior-law description: 50% deduction, partial alternative tax of 25% on the first $50,000). Cross-checked against Form 1040 instructions 1976 (https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-prior/i1040--1976.pdf): 50% of capital gain distributions taxable; alternative tax still computed on Schedule D. Minimum tax on preferences and maximum tax on earned income not modelled. Dividends were ordinary income (only the small dividend exclusion, ignored).	f
1977	exclusion	{"method":"","excludedShare":0.5,"alternativeRate":0.25,"alternativeLimit":{"single":50000,"marriedJoint":50000},"source":""}	https://www.jct.gov/getattachment/89bb713c-bb3f-4a1d-a987-425856365722/s-16-70-2406.pdf	JCS-16-70 p. 167: from 1972 the 25% alternative rate applies only to the first $50,000 of net long-term gain; gain above that gets the regular 50% deduction (top effective rate 35%, half of 70%). Rule unchanged until the Revenue Act of 1978 (JCS-7-79 prior-law description: 50% deduction, partial alternative tax of 25% on the first $50,000). Cross-checked against Form 1040 instructions 1977 (https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-prior/i1040--1977.pdf): 50% of capital gain distributions taxable; alternative tax still computed on Schedule D. Minimum tax on preferences and maximum tax on earned income not modelled. Dividends were ordinary income (only the small dividend exclusion, ignored).	f
1978	exclusion	{"method":"","excludedShare":0.5,"alternativeRate":0.25,"alternativeLimit":{"single":50000,"marriedJoint":50000},"source":""}	https://www.jct.gov/getattachment/39bea12f-bcf4-478b-9c02-d15552014bed/jcs-7-79-4156.pdf	JCT General Explanation of the Revenue Act of 1978 (JCS-7-79) pp. 251-254: prior law 50% deduction plus partial 25% alternative tax on the first $50,000; the Act raises the deduction to 60% for sales after October 31, 1978 (Nov-Dec 1978 sales: 60% exclusion, not modelled; rule covering most of the year used) and repeals the alternative tax after 1978. Form 1040 instructions 1978 (https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-prior/i1040--1978.pdf) p. 3 confirm the Nov. 1, 1978 change and that the alternative tax applies for 1978. Treasury OTA table lists max rate 39.875/33.85 for 1978. Dividends were ordinary income (only the small dividend exclusion, ignored).	f
1979	exclusion	{"method":"","excludedShare":0.6,"source":""}	https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-prior/i1040--1979.pdf	Form 1040 instructions 1979: 40% of capital gain distributions is taxable (60% excluded); 'No Alternative Tax Computation' (expired Dec 31, 1978). Matches JCS-7-79 (60% deduction, alternative tax repealed). The 60% deduction is a preference for the new alternative minimum tax (not modelled). Dividends were ordinary income (only the small dividend exclusion, ignored).	f
1980	exclusion	{"method":"","excludedShare":0.6,"source":""}	https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-prior/f1040sd--1980.pdf	Schedule D 1980 Part III: capital gain deduction is 60% of the net long-term gain (smaller of lines 18/19); no alternative tax part. AMT not modelled. Treasury OTA table max rate 28% (70% x 40%). Dividends were ordinary income (only the small dividend exclusion, ignored).	f
1981	exclusion	{"method":"","excludedShare":0.6,"alternativeRate":0.2,"source":""}	https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-prior/f1040sd--1981.pdf	Schedule D 1981: 60% deduction (line 21); Part IV 'Computation of Alternative Tax' for net gain from sales after June 9, 1981 (ERTA): tax on taxable income less 40% of that gain, plus 20% of that gain, with no dollar limit (so no alternativeLimit). Sales before June 10 (the smaller part of the year) got only the 60% deduction at rates up to 70% (28% effective). Treasury OTA table: max rate 28%/20% in 1981. Dividends were ordinary income (only the small dividend exclusion, ignored).	f
1982	exclusion	{"method":"","excludedShare":0.6,"source":""}	https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-prior/f1040sd--1982.pdf	Schedule D 1982 Part III: deduction of 60% of the net long-term gain; no alternative tax computation. With the 50% top rate the maximum effective rate is 20% (Treasury OTA table, https://home.treasury.gov/system/files/131/Taxes-Paid-on-Capital-Gains-for-Returns-with-Positive-Net-Capital-Gains-12202016.pdf, lists 20.00 for 1982). AMT not modelled. Dividends were ordinary income (only the small dividend exclusion, ignored).	f
